{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And Avraham fell on his face and laughed.</b> This is תִּ״אֻ, a term of joy — וַחֲדִי. And that of Sarah is a term of mockery. You learn that Avraham believed and rejoiced, while Sarah did not believe and scoffed — and this is why the Holy One, blessed be He, was indignant with Sarah and was not indignant with Avraham." "<b>Is it?</b> There are expressions of wonder that stand firm, such as ״הֲנִגְלֹה נִגְלֵיתִי?״ (I Samuel 2), ״הֲרֹאֶה אַתָּה?״ (Ezekiel 8) — this too stands firm. And so he said in his heart: Will such a kindness be done for another, that the Holy One, blessed be He, is doing for me?" "<b>And if Sarah, who is ninety years old.</b> She was fit to bear. And even though the early generations would beget children at five hundred years, by Avraham's time the years had already diminished and debility had come into the world. Go out and learn from the ten generations from Noach to Avraham — that their lifespans hastened to sixty and seventy."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The Aramaic particle תִּ״אֻ appears to be a manuscript abbreviation, likely for תֵּימַר אִתְיֵי or similar; rendered here as 'this is' based on context."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:06.438400621Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}